3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-13}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1235af24-4bb5-5ea7-b9aa-86848a4558cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418729 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=7,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{1}+{3}^{1}=7.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={4}^{k}+{3}^{k}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k} & =4({4}^{k}+{3}^{k})-{3}^{k} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{k}+4\\cdot {3}^{k}-{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=59,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using strong induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90b99752-34a5-5bfa-9d99-9e9bd7bb0b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418730 #edb/ref 1074 "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={8}^{1}-5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={8}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={8}^{k}-5 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={8}^{k-1}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={8}^{k+1}-5.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-10{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=21,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n},\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1} & =7({{field-14}})-10({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =5({{field-16}})-2({{field-17}}) \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-19}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac24346d-5ce4-50ce-a3b5-301fb4761c13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418731 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-10{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{1}-{2}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=21,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}=21.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={5}^{k}-{2}^{k} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={5}^{k-1}-{2}^{k-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={5}^{k+1}-{2}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1} & =7({5}^{k}-{2}^{k})-10({5}^{k-1}-{2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-10({5}^{k-1})+10({2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-2\\cdot 5({5}^{k-1})+5\\cdot 2({2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-2({5}^{k})+5({2}^{k}) \\\\  & =5({5}^{k})-2({2}^{k}) \\\\  & ={5}^{k+1}-{2}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186418734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 